 i'm going to  go into the general  design of what my solution was we'll  talk for a while about the  implementation and some of the  challenges that came up  through that and then finally we'll talk  about how it actually went and  what we can learn from the process  so first off the problem of predicting  voter turnout in a pandemic  i think i'll start by talking about what  normally happens on election night  um and pardon the interruption if you  see your hearing corgi  who is uh thinking why am i on a call  during her playtime  um but we'll try to power through here  uh so on election night  generally what happens is that local  election officials  tally votes at the precinct level and  report their results  up to the counties and states and then  these results go  to the associated press or another  organization  and they publish these combined tallies  that's where you you know if you go to  the new york times or washington post or  wherever  to see election results coming in  they're all sharing some of these same  sources  and the key metric for tracking how much  of the vote has been counted  is what's called precincts reported um  basically what will happen is that  you know of all these individual  election precincts  that report results um you know we know  the total number of precincts there are  and we know how many of them have called  in their results  so we can basically just use that  proportion as a proxy for what  percentage of the vote has been counted  and you know this is an approximation  it's not perfect  um for one these precincts aren't all  the same size so  you know one percent of precincts it  doesn't actually mean one percent of  votes but it's close enough  it's a good enough approximation  likewise early and absentee votes  they're often counted and reported  differently from election day results  different cities and counties and states  handle these things and others  differently  um some might for example count your  absentee votes at your local precinct  others might count them at some central  county election office  um and you know what's more too is the  order in which these are reported  is is not random so for example places  with higher turnout  um oftentimes in urban areas they'll  tend to report later  whereas the places with relatively few  voters can do their accounts pretty  quickly  all that said though in a normal  election year the percent of precincts  reporting  tends to be a pretty good approximation  of how much of the vote's been counted  so this is what it looks like in  practice and as a former dnc official  we're sort of revisiting some of my 2016  trauma here  um but bear with me this is from the new  york times  website um from their election coverage  in 2016.  and so you can see here you know i  pulled up the denver  results specifically um it shows 346 out  of 346 precincts reporting  across the state uh it says 100  reporting  that's simply based on that precincts  reported number this is what it looks  like in a normal year  and that gives you a good sense of how  many votes are actually in compared to  how many there are going to be  so the problem with 2020 um the biggest  one  that came up well obviously there were a  lot of problems with 2020 but  the biggest one that came up in terms of  vote counting is that  almost two-thirds of the votes were  actually cast before election day  uh the previous high was about a third  in the last presidential election  and more than a third of the total votes  were cast by mail  and so part of the problem here is that  you know i mentioned a few minutes ago  that  those votes often aren't counted at the  same time has  um the in-person votes on election day  and sometimes not in the same places not  at the precinct level um what also often  happens  is that these are processed afterwards  um in some states for example  um this came up in pennsylvania early  votes aren't allowed to be counted  until the polls are already closed so  what might happen is that  you know you would get say voting  machines results almost instantaneously  after the polling  closes but you actually would have to  open all of these envelopes  uh and then process the paper apps and t  ballots one at a time  so those can take a lot longer we saw  you know during  well not election night election week  this uh last year  that pennsylvania actually took all the  way to you know the weekend in order to  be able to get all of those votes  counted  um also you know one of the problems  that we ran into was that the votes  didn't even have to be  in to the the state or county  by election day in many places that in a  lot of cases when the rules were relaxed  to allow easier vote by mail one of the  things that often got moved was the  requirement that votes  arrive by election day in a lot of  places what happened instead was  the votes had to be postmarked by  election day so that meant votes might  come in even  you know four five six days later um  i think some of them as much as a week  or two if they could come in  and so when two-thirds of the votes  aren't being cast  in the normal way on election day at  polling places suddenly that precincts  reporting estimate  isn't really a reliable indicator of how  much has been counted  so i got a call from bloomberg news to  help them  try to fix this problem for their  election night coverage  so the associated press had you know  told its members that  they were going to be putting out these  overall turnout predictions  for every state um but they hadn't said  you know how these are going to be done  and they weren't making any promises  about doing it at the county level  or for congressional districts or for  any race other than the presidential  election  so that's what bloomberg news reached  out to me for um  you know they knew me from from  reputation from my past work with the  dnc and elsewhere  and so you know i agreed with them to  put together  uh predictions for each state for each  county for each  congressional district in the case of  congressional races and for presidential  senate house and gubernatorial races so  these would what these would be what  bloomberg news used on election night  in their coverage instead of the  precincts reporting measure  so to do this i designed a voter turnout  forecast model  and before we get to the details of the  model i think it's worth pointing out  that making a model like this  has some fundamental problems that you  need to wrestle with  the biggest one more than anything else  is that an election is a one-time event  that you know yes we have presidential  elections every four years  but they're not the same thing there are  different candidates different contexts  this year there was last year there's a  pandemic you know  these kinds of things happen and so um  what often  happens here is that you know we try to  proxy  by looking at previous selections as  a a a baseline but it's not the same  thing so  particularly in a year where it's just a  fundamentally different environment than  any that's come before  it gets really hard to come up with  accurate prediction  and what's more contrary to what 538  the economist and other you know  predictions might tell you  you can't actually quantify the  uncertainty of a one-time event  this is a sort of a philosophical  question but it's it's impossible  because  you know you can't if something happens  right  if an event happens all you know about  the probability of that event happening  is that it wasn't zero that's all you  can say  um you can't say whether the probability  of that event happening was a hundred  percent  or point zero zero zero one percent we  just happened to get to that one  um it's really impossible to say when  you only have one observation  so you can try to estimate it from other  similar events but that's not the same  thing  and likewise um the other problem that  you run into by using those past events  that are similar  is that you're relying on having  comparable data and that the data  generating mechanisms  that produce that are are comparable so  for example um just as a starting point  yes we've had you know over 50  presidential elections in this country  but  we don't have all the same data going  back that far  um in fact you know just getting county  level presidential election  returns um you can only do that for the  past 20 years  and and other data sources for example  the american community survey  basically what the census bureau  produces um  in years that aren't divisible by 10 to  say how many people there are and what  their demographics are and so forth  you know that doesn't go back to 2000  its current format you know it's i mean  it's change it changes a little bit from  year to year but the current format of  the acs  really only goes back about a decade  likewise  um the district boundaries for  congressional districts even  the the counties in the country they do  change over time so again it's really  hard to get  comparable data that lines up um and  then when you're talking about rules  things like uh you know who's eligible  to vote absentee  how much early voting there is um  certain states now  do all vote by mail where you know  that's a  rapidly growing thing but that wasn't  the case 20 years ago  um so you have to account for all of  these things and it's really hard to get  all of the data line up  and then finally like i said 2020 was a  very unusual year for many reasons  and so you know expecting the uh the  patterns you saw  in previous elections to hold is a bit  of a challenge  so my goal here wasn't to come up with a  perfect solution it's one that  was good enough and and this is sort of  a philosophical approach i have about  these kinds of projects  then i'm not going for a perfect  solution i'm going for a solution  that's better than whatever it is we'd  have if i didn't  do the work so in this case you know  maybe that solution  is the aps forecast which doesn't get at  the level of granularity we want  or you know it's going to be something  that 538 produces or somebody else or  maybe they're just going to look at you  know the last election results and say  well it'll be something more than that  most likely  um in any case you know i thought well i  can't do better or i can't do a perfect  thing  but i can do better than those so it's  actually really freeing to think about  it that way to realize that  you know you're never gonna have a  perfect solution but you can always make  it better  and so that's my approach to essentially  start with some kind of baseline  um you know in this case i took simply  the the  turnout rates we saw in each county and  state  from previous elections and then you  know i thought what can i add to that to  make it  um more accurate over time so  one thing to remember here too is that  data is always going to be messy and  this very much was i'll get into that  uh in a minute um but perhaps most  importantly of any of these things i  think it's also really critical  to be honest with yourself and with  everybody else about  what you don't know or what you can't  know and and plan to work around that  so translating all these philosophical  approaches into  practical ideas for this project um  first off  you know i had to real really make some  trade-offs about the amount of data i  could use  versus how accurate that was so for  example um you know i ended up only  going back to 2004 with presidential  results  i could have actually gone back to the  1970s if i wanted to use state level  um but you know that only gives us about  50 data points a year rather than three  thousand like we did with counties  um and you know i wanted i had to leave  2000 out  only because basically we're using the  lagged um turnout rate from the previous  election  as one of the inputs so we didn't have  that for 96  which meant we couldn't use 2