ifferent  turnout level scenarios and the idea was  that on election night we could actually  use the incoming data that we were  getting  from the states and counties to say at  what point should we switch from one  forecast to another  so the final design of the model that i  created um  the most important model was the model  of the county level presidential turnout  rates  so looking at at the number of votes as  a function  of the citizen voting age population and  so  the inputs to this i mentioned already  uh turn on previous  mid midterm and presidential elections i  also brought in a lot of information  about voting rules  um things like voter id and whether  felons are disenfranchised and so forth  there's a whole set of different  demographics that are relevant here and  you know a lot of this is based actually  in the work that i've done  back in my academic days you know  studying what are the things that  predict whether individual voters are  likely to turn out  and then i also brought in a lot of  information about the competitiveness  of the presidential race in each state  and about races for senate and governor  as well again  under you know the pretty well  established finding that the more  competitive  a specific race the more people are  likely to turn out  there's a second set of models as well  about what's called down ballot drop-off  this is when people show up they'll cast  a vote for president but they won't vote  for  senate or governor or house of  representatives  they'll only vote at the top of the  ticket and so i made a second set of  models  predicting this drop off as a function  of the competitiveness of those down  ballot rates  races a few voting rules and  socioeconomic status again things that  are established in the political science  research has pretty strong predictors of  behavior  so i mentioned earlier that i validated  these models by holding out  one year's elections at a time and then  finally what i did was i retrained the  models on all the all four elections  and predicted for the 2020 context and  then made the adjustments  to produce those high and historic level  forecasts  that i alluded to in the previous slide  and i'll get into a little more detail  about those as we go on  so now a bit about the implementation so  probably half the work on this project  was really just about finding and  compiling the right data  so you know one of the main data sources  that i started from  was election result data that was  compiled at mit  which has the most comprehensive and  granular  election result data  and you know in this data this was the  data set that  we get county data um for presidential  elections going back to 2000  um you could also get state data for  senate and gubernatorial races  uh congressional district level data for  congressional races  um you know what i found with this is  it's generally  very comprehensive very high quality  but that said you know there are issues  in there i found for example in some  cases um you know the definitions of  counties didn't line up because  certain results were reported um for  example in virginia where they often  would have  a sort of joint county where they have a  city  and the area around it um they're  technically different counties but they  report their  their election results together um those  kind of things came up i found a few  just  basic math errors that i don't know  where they came from but the numbers  were just  off um thankfully i did some validation  that helped me find  hopefully most of those but there were  some cases too where i had to dig into  places like ballotpedia and wikipedia  to try to pull out some more some other  sources of information  i mentioned the demographic data from  the american community survey  the census bureau also puts out the  current population survey which provides  some  similar data at different levels and  frequencies  i pulled competitiveness from a bunch of  different sources  the the more recent stuff um it's pretty  easy to get from places like 538 with  all their polling archives  um ballotpedia is really good at  compiling data  on uh race competitiveness rankings  from um you know a few different uh  sort of mainstream sources like the cook  political report  that's a pretty standard one that goes  back a while that said though  you know those data sources generally  start out  um around 2012 or so that's when those  those organizations started compiling  that prior to that i actually had to go  and do things like  look up old um newspaper articles  from say you know 2000 or 2004 or  whatever  um to try to find you know what for  example the cook ratings were  back in 2004 um because they're not  available online anywhere  you know those sorts of things i had to  go back and and dig around to find  likewise i also had to go to a lot of  different sources to get information  about voting rules  again this is one of those things where  it's not too hard to get current data  but when you start trying to roll back  in time that's where it gets tough i had  to go and research and say you know for  example  um when did a certain state start  requiring voter id  so that i can code you know the  elections before then as not requiring  it  but the elections afterwards is  requiring it um because that's one of  the things that we're thinking about in  terms of what predicts  overall turnout rates it's not only you  know what the rules are now but what  they were  at the time of a previous election and  even how they had changed in the run-up  to that election  so all that's to say getting data that's  consistent across years  was a real struggle and you know one of  the things that i i wrestled with  is the subjectivity in deciding when  data is good enough and when it's not  um you know i feel like if you had a  hundred people do this project  you would have a hundred very different  projects because i think everybody  will have different views about you know  how to make these trade-offs  and there's no one right or wrong answer  there's no standard for how to do this  um ultimately this is the sort of art of  the art and science of this kind of  project  so i did most of this work in jupiter um  i think a lot of you are familiar with  jupiter notebooks  and if you're really a big fan  of jupiter workflows i did a jupiter con  presentation a few years back on  um using jupiter notebooks and  production and  kind of how i approach that but just to  really kind of summarize how i do a lot  of projects  normally um the way i start and i'm  speaking as really a data scientist  not an engineer so um you know i'm sure  a lot of software engineers would  would cringe at what i do um at the  other end of the spectrum i think a lot  of you know researcher types  wouldn't get past the v 0.1 so i'm okay  with being in the middle there  um but so what i started doing is doing  everything in linear code  you know i do things one at a time in  either one or many notebooks  and then the next step what i'll do is  i'll take things that generalize  particularly around things like building  models i'll move them  into functions within the notebook and  i'll start pulling out the parameters  put them say up at a block at the top of  the notebook so i can  play around with them as needed without  having to dig through all the code  the v 0.3 is often moving those  functions into modules and importing  them  basically that's what i do once i get  those functions in a place where i'm  happy with them and don't need to make  changes  the optional v04 is maybe i just move  everything into a standard python script  i mentioned that jupiter con  presentation a minute ago i find this  part to be more and more optional over  time because you can actually use  notebooks in a production setting if  you're not familiar with something  called nb convert  i i would advise you to look into it um  if you're interested because  what it allows you to do is to schedule  a notebook run where  you don't actually have to be doing it  manually it can run on a  cron job or an airflow or whatever and  you can get all the  same outputs you would as if you had it  open in the browser and then finally i  move everything to production  but so for this one uh this was a sort  of  fast-paced project you know i had about  six weeks to put it together  i was also working alone and this was  something that you know hopefully i  wouldn't have to do again anytime soon  so i went with the sort of hybrid hybrid  approach um hybrid of  0.1 for the brand new code and 0.3 for  things i've adapted from  other projects um you know like a lot of  you i'm sure  over time i've realized i do a lot of  the same things the same way  particularly when it comes to machine  learning um so i got a pretty well built  out code base of  you know how i do things like model  tuning that i can just reuse for  different projects  um you know it wasn't the cleanest most  elegant approach like i said a lot of  software engineers might object  um it worked great for me and especially  to uh  needing to move quickly i felt like that  strike the right balance that said  um you know this is one that i was  working alone on  and it wasn't something that i was going  to need to replicate later so  if those are considerations you know  maybe try to be a little more deliberate  in your workflow  so one thing i did a lot of in this was  ensembling  so this is a common thing too when i'm  making models  for those of you who aren't familiar  with ensembling generally the basic  thinking is that you know every model  has weaknesses  um but by combining different algorithms  the idea is hopefully  by averaging those predictions together  you can cancel out some of the  weaknesses and build a model  that's better than what you can get from  any individual algorithm  so the final presidential turnout models  we used  um they used three different tree based  algorithms random forests extra trees  and gradient boosted decision trees  and the idea behind this was just that  each of these algorithms handles the  input features in a slightly different  way  um for example you know random forest  regressions  they tend to give more weight than extra  trees regressions do  to um continuous features because of the  way they select where to split  um make splits within your data set  um i could talk for hours about the the  logistics of that just suffice it to say  that you know they tend to focus a  little more on continuous  variables whereas extra trees models  tend to go a little more towards the  binary variables and so  the idea of combining them together is  that you know you give different  features a chance to shine in each in  each model  so that you know none of them just  happens to not really get much attention  simply because  the particular algorithm you used just  really doesn't have much of a taste for  that kind of feature  um i also tried using linear regression  and various kinds of regularization  but i found actually in the testing they  made the ensemble predictions worse  um tree models tend to be really  flexible um  you know regression based or linear  regression based models  aren't very flexible um and so  you know you either have to build in a  lot of interactions manually  or else you just have to accept that um  you know  interactions between the different  inputs aren't going to be accounted for  and so that made the predictions  substantially worse i ended up excluding  them at the end  the one exception was for the down  ballot models  in those cases we had a much smaller  sample um  you know for the senate and governor  races we were doing it at the state  level  so only a couple hundred data points  and i apologize if you hear my dog  squeaking her toy she does this when i'm  on zoom sometimes  um so you know we had a few we had a lot  fewer data samples  um we had fewer featu