es at your local precinct  others might count them at some central  county election office  um and you know what's more too is the  order in which these are reported  is is not random so for example places  with higher turnout  um oftentimes in urban areas they'll  tend to report later  whereas the places with relatively few  voters can do their accounts pretty  quickly  all that said though in a normal  election year the percent of precincts  reporting  tends to be a pretty good approximation  of how much of the vote's been counted  so this is what it looks like in  practice and as a former dnc official  we're sort of revisiting some of my 2016  trauma here  um but bear with me this is from the new  york times  website um from their election coverage  in 2016.  and so you can see here you know i  pulled up the denver  results specifically um it shows 346 out  of 346 precincts reporting  across the state uh it says 100  reporting  that's simply based on that precincts  reported number this is what it looks  like in a normal year  and that gives you a good sense of how  many votes are actually in compared to  how many there are going to be  so the problem with 2020 um the biggest  one  that came up well obviously there were a  lot of problems with 2020 but  the biggest one that came up in terms of  vote counting is that  almost two-thirds of the votes were  actually cast before election day  uh the previous high was about a third  in the last presidential election  and more than a third of the total votes  were cast by mail  and so part of the problem here is that  you know i mentioned a few minutes ago  that  those votes often aren't counted at the  same time has  um the in-person votes on election day  and sometimes not in the same places not  at the precinct level um what also often  happens  is that these are processed afterwards  um in some states for example  um this came up in pennsylvania early  votes aren't allowed to be counted  until the polls are already 