dictions for each state for each  county for each  congressional district in the case of  congressional races and for presidential  senate house and gubernatorial races so  these would what these would be what  bloomberg news used on election night  in their coverage instead of the  precincts reporting measure  so to do this i designed a voter turnout  forecast model  and before we get to the details of the  model i think it's worth pointing out  that making a model like this  has some fundamental problems that you  need to wrestle with  the biggest one more than anything else  is that an election is a one-time event  that you know yes we have presidential  elections every four years  but they're not the same thing there are  different candidates different contexts  this year there was last year there's a  pandemic you know  these kinds of things happen and so um  what often  happens here is that you know we try to  proxy  by looking at previous selections as  a a a baseline but it's not the same  thing so  particularly in a year where it's just a  fundamentally different environment than  any that's come before  it gets really hard to come up with  accurate prediction  and what's more contrary to what 538  the economist and other you know  predictions might tell you  you can't actually quantify the  uncertainty of a one-time event  this is a sort of a philosophical  question but it's it's impossible  because  you know you can't if something happens  right  if an event happens all you know about  the probability of that event happening  is that it wasn't zero that's all you  can say  um you can't say whether the probability  of that event happening was a hundred  percent  or point zero zero zero one percent we  just happened to get to that one  um it's really impossible to say when  you only have one observation  so you can try to estimate it from other  similar events but that's not the same  thing  and likewise um the other problem that  you run into by using those past events  that are similar  is that you're relying on having  comparable data and that the data  generating mechanisms  that produce that are are comparable so  for example um just as a starting point  yes we've had you know over 50  presidential elections in this country  but  we don't have all the same data going  back that far  um in fact you know just getting county  level presidential election  returns um you can only do that for the  past 20 years  and and other data sources for example  the american community survey  basically what the census bureau  produces um  in years that aren't divisible by 10 to  say how many people there are and what  their demographics are and so forth  you know that doesn't go back to 2000  its current format you know it's i mean  it's change it changes a little bit from  year to year but the current format of  the acs  really only goes back about a decade  likewise  um the district boundaries for  congressional districts even  the the counties in the country they do  change over time so again it's really  hard to get  comparable data that lines up um and  then when you're talking about rules  things like uh you know who's eligible  to vote absentee  how much early voting there is um  certain states now  do all vote by mail where you know  that's a  rapidly growing thing but that wasn't  the case 20 years ago  um so you have to account for all of  these things and it's really hard to get  all of the data line up  and then finally like i said 2020 was a  very unusual year for many reasons  and so you know expecting the uh the  patterns you saw  in previous elections to hold is a bit  of a challenge  so my goal here wasn't to come up with a  perfect solution it's one that  was good enough and and this is sort of  a philosophical approach i have about  these kinds of projects  then i'm not going for a perfect  solution i'm going for a solution  that's better than whatever it is we'd  have if i didn't  do the work so in this case y