ifferent  turnout level scenarios and the idea was  that on election night we could actually  use the incoming data that we were  getting  from the states and counties to say at  what point should we switch from one  forecast to another  so the final design of the model that i  created um  the most important model was the model  of the county level presidential turnout  rates  so looking at at the number of votes as  a function  of the citizen voting age population and  so  the inputs to this i mentioned already  uh turn on previous  mid midterm and presidential elections i  also brought in a lot of information  about voting rules  um things like voter id and whether  felons are disenfranchised and so forth  there's a whole set of different  demographics that are relevant here and  you know a lot of this is based actually  in the work that i've done  back in my academic days you know  studying what are the things that  predict whether individual voters are  likely to turn out  and then i also brought in a lot of  information about the competitiveness  of the presidential race in each state  and about races for senate and governor  as well again  under you know the pretty well  established finding that the more  competitive  a specific race the more people are  likely to turn out  there's a second set of models as well  about what's called down ballot drop-off  this is when people show up they'll cast  a vote for president but they won't vote  for  senate or governor or house of  representatives  they'll only vote at the top of the  ticket and so i made a second set of  models  predicting this drop off as a function  of the competitiveness of those down  ballot rates  races a few voting rules and  socioeconomic status again things that  are established in the political science  research has pretty strong predictors of  behavior  so i mentioned earlier that i validated  these models by holding out  one year's elections at a time and then  finally what i did was i retr