e different views about you know  how to make these trade-offs  and there's no one right or wrong answer  there's no standard for how to do this  um ultimately this is the sort of art of  the art and science of this kind of  project  so i did most of this work in jupiter um  i think a lot of you are familiar with  jupiter notebooks  and if you're really a big fan  of jupiter workflows i did a jupiter con  presentation a few years back on  um using jupiter notebooks and  production and  kind of how i approach that but just to  really kind of summarize how i do a lot  of projects  normally um the way i start and i'm  speaking as really a data scientist  not an engineer so um you know i'm sure  a lot of software engineers would  would cringe at what i do um at the  other end of the spectrum i think a lot  of you know researcher types  wouldn't get past the v 0.1 so i'm okay  with being in the middle there  um but so what i started doing is doing  everything in linear code  you know i do things one at a time in  either one or many notebooks  and then the next step what i'll do is  i'll take things that generalize  particularly around things like building  models i'll move them  into functions within the notebook and  i'll start pulling out the parameters  put them say up at a block at the top of  the notebook so i can  play around with them as needed without  having to dig through all the code  the v 0.3 is often moving those  functions into modules and importing  them  basically that's what i do once i get  those functions in a place where i'm  happy with them and don't need to make  changes  the optional v04 is maybe i just move  everything into a standard python script  i mentioned that jupiter con  presentation a minute ago i find this  part to be more and more optional over  time because you can actually use  notebooks in a production setting if  you're not familiar with something  called nb convert  i i would advise you to look into it um  if you're interested bec