ent tree based  algorithms random forests extra trees  and gradient boosted decision trees  and the idea behind this was just that  each of these algorithms handles the  input features in a slightly different  way  um for example you know random forest  regressions  they tend to give more weight than extra  trees regressions do  to um continuous features because of the  way they select where to split  um make splits within your data set  um i could talk for hours about the the  logistics of that just suffice it to say  that you know they tend to focus a  little more on continuous  variables whereas extra trees models  tend to go a little more towards the  binary variables and so  the idea of combining them together is  that you know you give different  features a chance to shine in each in  each model  so that you know none of them just  happens to not really get much attention  simply because  the particular algorithm you used just  really doesn't have much of a taste for  that kind of feature  um i also tried using linear regression  and various kinds of regularization  but i found actually in the testing they  made the ensemble predictions worse  um tree models tend to be really  flexible um  you know regression based or linear  regression based models  aren't very flexible um and so  you know you either have to build in a  lot of interactions manually  or else you just have to accept that um  you know  interactions between the different  inputs aren't going to be accounted for  and so that made the predictions  substantially worse i ended up excluding  them at the end  the one exception was for the down  ballot models  in those cases we had a much smaller  sample um  you know for the senate and governor  races we were doing it at the state  level  so only a couple hundred data points  and i apologize if you hear my dog  squeaking her toy she does this when i'm  on zoom sometimes  um so you know we had a few we had a lot  fewer data samples  um we had fewer features and simpler  specifications  so for those i just use linear  regression for simplicity  but for the much larger presidential  turnout models that's where we actually  use  all of the tree based models so  there's a lot of tuning that went on in  this um  you know i spent a lot of time manually  collecting and refining the data but  what that actually translated to in the  modeling  wasn't so much setting up the model that  took time as figuring out  you know which which algorithms to use  how to choose the right set of features  to include  there's a lot of tuning and retraining  also when i went back and i would pull  in  you know a slightly improved set of data  after i did some  some error checking and so forth and the  basic setup here of  you know what went into this just the  presidential models we had about three  thousand a little over three thousand  counties  four years of data um depending on the  run anywhere from 50 to 100 features  three to five algorithms at a time we  were tuning  three to five hyper parameters per  algorithm  we were doing four validations sets of  models plus  the final set of models all that those  numbers don't really mean much  other than say there was a lot of  horsepower that we had to use to  to compute this and the laptop i got a  few years ago  um it was meant so i could put in a bag  back when we used to leave the house and  go work in other places  which is and it's great for that it's  not that powerful though and so  the solution i found um was actually to  use a tool called desk  so if you're not familiar with das dasc  is a python library  that basically allows you to distribute  data tasks  across different workers and in  particular where it shines is  doing it when you're distributing across  a cluster um  on more than one machine you could also  use it for parallelizing things on a  single machine  it's a good use case for that but in  this case you know deploying on a  cluster was really what i was going for 