d  validating the models where i held out  one year each  and so there were two purposes for this  one was just to make sure that the  models actually worked as intended and  that you know  i was able to produce useful forecasts  um but more importantly i also wanted to  get a good sense of what the uncertainty  in the predictions is was likely to be  when i made a model of  of other years than the one i was  testing it  um so basically this allowed us to look  both  you know how much variance do we expect  to see in our predictions  across counties or across states but  also overall um  because that's the really hard thing and  the hard thing that i was  worried a lot about when it came to  2020. um  how do those year by year patterns  change so  on that last point looking at the four  different years  um basically it ranged from our  predictions being about  three and a half percent too low um  in 2004 to 16.3 percent too high in  2012.  um which kind of makes sense you know  for example the 2012 one in particular  um 2008 was a really um high turnout  year  compared to some of the previous ones in  2012  we did see turnout drop pretty  substantially  from from 2008. so remember i mentioned  that  you know the past elections turnout um  was one of the big inputs well it makes  sense then that  you know if that's our starting point um  and turnout goes down pretty  substantially that we are going to see  um you know our forecast coming in a bit  high  so then going down to look at the more  granular level um  this is where i really want to just get  a sense of like how close  were we overall so there are always  going to be outliers when you do these  things  um and i looked a lot at the outliers to  see you know where their  particular patterns um that were coming  up you know places  for example one of the things that i did  as a result of  um doing some of that outlier checking  was to actually  incorporate as a feature um the growth  rate of the population in a