 the result will fall within that range  and and  to you know give a bit of a spoiler they  did it worked out  um one important thing we did here was  actually applied the same range  at all levels so this is something that  um  really gets to the structure of errors  and  for me this is um particularly important  because when you see  something like a polling forecast um  what oftentimes pulling forecasts rely  on and this was one of the big problems  in 2016  why so many forecasts got it wrong is  they assume that  you know by averaging different polls  together um  the errors will kind of cancel each  other out and you won't have what are  called correlated errors which is that  you know all the polls may be wrong in  the same direction  um by applying the same range at the  county level the state level and the  national level  what we said was basically that you know  we're saying  the same uncertainty level at all levels  meaning that um  we might be you know wrong in the same  direction every time  and that's okay we'll still you know  we're not going to assume these things  are just going to cancel out  but the other thing we did too is i  mentioned before that we calculated  three different  types of forecasts based on three  different levels of turnout  so our baseline forecast was just on our  model's predictions  so what that said was let's take you  know the average of the trends we saw  across  across the past four elections not just  average the turnout levels but the sort  of turnout patterns generally  and adjust them by changes in for  example the rules around voting so  you know when there's more absentee and  early voting  that tends to increase um turnout rates  so we did allow for that and so you know  we were looking at  a an increase in the turnout rate over  the average of the past four  um four cycles but you know fairly  modest one  but you know that said we were looking  at the number i believe it was something  like 142 million  um our ba