  um and you know generally 2008 was a  pretty high fork  or a high turnout election going back  you know the past  several decades so we said okay you know  the difference between a  a very a high turnout election and a  normal one it's roughly five percent  again this is kind of arbitrary because  at the end of the day we don't  this isn't something where we can we can  estimate precisely so we're not even  going to try  instead we said you know looking at the  data that seems like the difference  based on historical patterns of between  a normal election and a high turnout one  but then looking at at this year or at  2020  there were a lot of signs that it was  actually going to be more than that um  you know in particular looking at the  rate of early and absentee voting  um i showed earlier that you know we had  over 100 million votes cast before  election day  um and that's more than double we'd ever  seen in the past  um and obviously a lot of those voters  were people who otherwise would have  turned out on election day  but still um you know that was a really  strong sign the enthusiasm was very high  likewise all the other signs you know  pointed the same direction  so we also decided to come up with this  historic forecast which simply takes  the high turnout forecast adds another  five percent to it  um and decided essentially that we're  going to  you know start with the baseline  forecast and then move from one to the  next to the next throughout election  night  based on the data that comes in and  finally um the other thing we did before  we  released was we wrote up a pretty  extensive methodology document  and published it for everybody to see so  you know one of the things we were  trying to do here was to be really  transparent about what we were doing  what the inputs were um give some sense  of what the uncertainty all meant  you know this was a pretty complex  project and i wanted to make sure that  um well i wasn't going to get to the  level of you know  making it totally academic um i wanted  to make it so that a you know  sophisticated reader would get a good  sense of  what they were looking at  so finally the last section before we  wrap up  what happened so on election night  the plan was you know we're going to  load up our baseline forecast  and for each as i said before for each  state and county  we'll be showing a range of the total  votes we expect  and so the plan was that you know we  have these high  and historic turnout forecasts and what  we did is we set some  conditions for when we're going to  switch from one to the other and that's  based on  you know certain number of counties or a  certain number of states  crossing um the upper bound of the range  of whatever  turnout forecast we're using right now  um you know we presume we're only going  to go  up from uh the baseline um we did set  some conditions for when we might roll  back but  we didn't expect to have to use that and  then on the site itself what it would  show  is the counting progress for votes as a  percentage  of the expected number of votes under  the forecast that is it  ends up becoming a range of percentages  as i'll show in a minute  um and we also make a point of  explaining which forecast  we're using at any given time um that's  why you know if you looked at one point  in time and saw  oh we were you know somewhere between  say 80 and 80  and 90 percent of the votes counted and  then suddenly  you looked again and it said 75 to 85  the reason would  be if you looked at it that oh we've  switched from one forecast to another  so first off the national tracker this  is what was on the front page of the  site  you know it had um like you've seen a  lot of places you know it'll have all  the states  you can mouse over it and pull up the  state-specific info  they also had a tracker to look at the  um  the progress of counting votes overall  um and to look at the sort of partisan  composition  within each of those um they also did  some cool things around  you know those lines on the right um the  reason the colors change  over the course of it is so they can  kind of track the progress  of um votes that come in so for example  if you look at say um nevada you know it  started out very red  um because clark county which is where  las vegas is the really blue part of the  state  that counted more slowly than the you  know more rural parts and so  the votes that came in late were much  more democratic  than the early ones anyways you can kind  of get a sense here too  these aren't the final results this  stopped updating before  every state had finalized its vote  counting um but this is close to it you  can kind of  get a sense just looking at this um how  close we were to the  100 of the forecast um and this is  i'll mention a minute i'll get into this  in a minute this is the historic turnout  forecast  which we we got to uh very late on  election night  um but you'll see you know for on  average we were  just about in the right territory there  were some places that were a little high  or a little low  um overall it worked out pretty well and  when you looked at  individual states here's georgia for  example um the way you would see it  is you know they would show the results  and the number of votes counted  but then again show you know how many  votes have count  have been counted and then what the  range of expected votes here  is so in this case georgia there were 5  million votes in total  the range of this historic voter turnout  forecast  was 4.78 to 5.28 million  um which means you know the the the mean  of that is 5.03 so we were dead on  in in georgia um unfortunately we were  really close in a lot of places  particularly  places that turned out to be really  close  the problem with florida um i mean i  kind of laughed when i wrote this  because  i could come up with many issues to take  up with florida but  um in this case actually it's not  florida's fault they actually did  something right  um which was they reported their vote  counts super early  and it was really frustrating because  what was happening was  there was super high turnout in florida  and they actually were one of the  the states that really over performed  relative to our forecasts  um but you know by pretty early in the  evening you know around 9 00 p.m  most of the places were were fully  reporting um and at that point in the  night you know no other state  was anywhere close to as um you know as  as  complete as they were and they were  getting a lot of attention  um particularly because it came in much  more republican than expected  and so we were still on our baseline  forecast and we're seeing some counties  coming in as  120 of our forecast or more um  and so you know the the team that i was  working with at bloomberg graphics  um they were getting calls from the news  desk saying hey why the hell is our our  forecast so far off  and then they were asking me and i said  well we have these rules and we're not  there yet  um and so we're really stressing out  about this um  we hadn't met the trigger for going to  high we did eventually  um just you know later that evening  switch it up there i don't know  i think we got close to the actual  trigger we might have even crossed it  but um  you know we at some point we just  realized yes it's obviously gonna get  there  um and you know in hindsight we'd  probably adjust the rules a little bit  but overall it worked it was a little  slower than expected um we also switched  to the historic forecast  around 5 a.m or so um i don't remember  exactly when it was some time before  dawn  because i started i remember going to  bed around the time the sun came up that  morning  um and then waking up a couple hours  later and getting back online but  um yeah you know we made that switch we  ended up getting to that historic  forecast  and ultimately i'm glad we did um just  to contrast  the florida example though um the  problem with new york  has their own folks say this is city and  state which is one of the new york  political  magazines um they described it as a  national embarrassment  because even weeks after the votes were  cast in new york they still hadn't  finished counting  um they even managed to miss legal  deadlines to do so  um and this is true for several states  so that's why uh you know when when you  look at the site as it is now  um it's actually showing that we only  got to about 96  of the forecast in in nationally um and  that's because  you know then when they stopped it about  a month after the election um not all  the votes were in in places like new  york were actually still reporting  so overall uh we're actually pretty much  right on target  so the final vote count was just a  little under 160 million  our forecast was 161. um the range was  you know 157 to 165 so  you know we were within basically a  percentage point um  of of the actual turnout number that's  our historic forecast you know  that's because we were able to adjust  with time  um but that was the point you know  worked as as expected  and what i think was really pleasing to  me was that in a lot of key swing states  um our forecast was similarly dead-on so  you know places like georgia michigan  nevada north carolina  we were within one percent um places a  couple places like pennsylvania  wisconsin we were a couple points off  but not far  um we did see some places where we're  off by quite a bit  um places like ohio and or sorry not  ohio hawaii and idaho  um but that said you know and this was  kind of my focus i  didn't do anything with waiting to do  this but i definitely um made a point of  you know really focusing on the the data  checking on  um places that i expected to be swing  states um you know if i saw  a potential data issue in idaho i wasn't  going to waste a lot of time trying to  fix it  but if it was wisconsin i absolutely was  um  but likewise you know i think a lot of  this has to do with  uh turnout being more consistent in  places that are highly competitive  um so at the end of the day everything  was within plus or minus seven percent  of the historic forecast and and most  places were within  plus or minus five percent so you know  that's what we were hoping for and and  that's what we got  all right so finally um you know i was  really pleased with how this worked out  2020 was a hellish nightmare of a year  in a lot of ways um and i was very  pleasantly surprised to see that this  one thing actually went as well as i  could have expected  um but the real credit you know doesn't  go towards some  brilliant design um or magic it was  humility  the the humility of presenting forecasts  as ranges so you set  the expectation for the audience that  you're not going to be exactly right  every time  and this is one of the mistakes i think  that places like 538 have made  um you know in 2016 for example  you know they got a lot of heat as did  pretty much everybody else who was  aggregating polls  for you know missing the election  because they were off by a couple points  um you know that was partly  the fault of what i mentioned about um  those correlated errors  where you're getting a little too cocky  that everything's just gonna  you know even out when you aggregate all  this information together  and so in our case we presented these  ranges  to set the expectation clearly that yeah  we're probably going to be off by a few  points  here and there but that's okay that's  that's just the plan  um likewise we didn't assume that those  errors were going to average out  um we we presented you know as much  uncertainty at the national level as we  did for counties  and then we designed the system knowing  that it was impossible  to know everything in advance that 2020  was going to surprise us  and so rather than either pretend it  wasn't  or to throw up our hands what we said  was you know let's design a system  that allows us to adapt to the data  that's coming in  in a really basic way it wasn't  something like you know the new york  times needle or what washington post did  at the very end  where they tried to come up with some  dynamic forecasts  um remember the washington post they  didn't turn it on to like two days later  because it turns out you know those  models are really hard and i've done  that before  i did that at the dnc in 2014 i had no  uh no wish to ever do that again you  know so we did something really simple  which is  let's come up with a low medium and high  forecast and switch from one  to the other based on the totality of  the results that are coming in  and so that actually ended up being a  very simple  um but very effective solution to get  to you know the answer we needed which  was how many votes are going to be cast  um the end of the day we got there and  we answered the question we needed to  that said definitely room for  improvement um one thing  i would do differently is pay more  attention up front to data quality  and part of that might mean scaling back  my ambitions about what data i was going  to include  i think particularly a lot of the things  about voting roles  you know i i kind of felt like well we  need all the information about voter id  and felon disenfranchisement and  how many days of early voting there are  in every state in every election  and you know ultimately when i actually  ended up running the models  um most of those didn't prove that  valuable anyways  but it was a hell of a lot of work and  it could have introduced as much noise  as it took away i don't know  um but i think for the sake of doing  this more efficiently in the future i  think  you know paying attention to the data  that we know is quality would probably  be a better approach  um in terms of the adaptive forecast um  i mentioned  the the problem with florida and moving  from different level one level of  turnout to another  i think in the future i'd be a bit more  uh willing to take risks on that  um the problem we ran into with florida  was we didn't we didn't have a mechanism  to just say  hey there's one place that we think is a  good indicator of where everything's  going  so we're going to move the national  forecast based on that  um you know and in hindsight if i were  doing again i'd either  make the rules a lot more loose as to  when we do that or maybe  you know do it on say a state by state  basis so we could have  say flip florida to the historic  forecast  um early on in the night rather than  have to wait and kind of look silly for  a little while  um and then finally the other thing i do  was when i was going through this and  thinking about  you know sort of my philosophy  approaching these projects i realized i  did one thing that really goes against  the way that i like to do it and the way  i actually teach my students how to do  it  um because one thing i always like to  remind my students is that  you can always add complexity later it's  much harder to take it away and i think  i violated that  when i did this project i came up with  this really long list  of all the things i thought it should be  included um and i kind of started from  there and i took a few  out along the way once i realized say  that i couldn't get  some data set that i needed um but in  hindsight  if i were to do it again i'd probably go  the other way and i would start building  up more gradually because it's really  unclear to me how much of that  complexity was really necessary  i mentioned things like voting rules  where at the end of the day the features  actually didn't add a whole lot of value  and i think that's probably the case for  a lot of things for example  you know i mean i was able to look and  see  hey for example um you know adding  adding  uh additional algorithms to the  ensembles  you know yeah they improved overall  performance in terms of  my sort of key metrics but i didn't  really  dive in and see like is this a  meaningful difference and  is this slight you know improvement in r  squared or whatever metric i was using  um  you know is that really worth the added  complexity of having multiple algorithms  and having to retune everything  um you know you know i'd say maybe i'd  go back and try it a little more simply  see how well that performs and then only  add complexity  if it actually makes the model um  notably better in a really substantial  way  so that's all i've got um i've got a few  time a few minutes if anybody has any  questions i think i saw some pop up in  the chat  um you can always find me on twitter i'm  i'm very easy to find there  and and you'll find lots of uh corgi  photos  and burritos as well as snarky comments  about tech  you can also shoot me an email here and  i've posted the slides on my github  i did that right before this um so if  you want a copy just go there and grab  them  um you know and feel free to share or  send me an email if you have any  questions  with that um yeah i'll take a few  questions from the chat  uh all right there are a few uh lane  would like to know have you compared  coil to saturn cloud  no i haven't i haven't um not familiar  with saturn  i'm definitely actually going to write  that down because i'm curious  all right next one is from caesar can  you explain a bit more about how the  actual data was fed into the model  and the predicted data was read out of  the bottle on election night  yeah so we actually did this um so  i tried to keep it as simple as possible  and uh you know i mentioned  back in 2014 that i was doing uh  election forecasting for the the dnc  um this was basically a team of uh  five of my data scientists in in a roman  democratic party headquarters  and then a couple dozen people around  the country um  who worked for different firms and state  parties and  everybody kind of gathered together and  entering data and then re-running models  and my less the lesson that i took from  that was i never want to do anything  like that again  um and so for this one i actually kept  it really simple  so you know i brought in the data um  manually  you know literally went grabbed it from  whatever site  i could and saved it um there were some  cases like the census data where i did  use the api  and i just had a script where i went and  pulled it but i wasn't doing that  dynamically i wasn't doing that at the  time of model training  i just wrote a script to call it prep  the data  um so i you know got that done weeks in  advance and then on the output side  um again i i kept it as simple as  possible you know the  the bloomberg folks they had their back  end for the  application they were using but all i  did is i delivered them a giant  csv um or actually i guess three  i had i gave them the i think i gave  them a national forecast sorry three  there was a state forecast  a congressional district forecast and  county forecast  and all of them were csvs and then you  know every  data point for that forecast was a  separate columns there's a column  for the you know upper and lower bounds  of each of the forecasts as well as the  midpoint um  and you know i gave that to them four  days in advance  they got it loaded and so on election  night when we were switching from one  forecast to the other  literally all i had to do was send an  email and said hey switch to the high  forecast  and then somebody on the application  team just re-pointed to a different  column  uh okay that last question was actually  from brent  andrew caesar's real question was andrew  did you consider using  campaign advertising spend on the model  yes  yes um definitely something i was  looking into um this kind of gets to  that  question of comparable data from year  over year  um and this is one where i really  struggled this was  if i had um so i originally specced out  this project around the beginning of  august  but the contract didn't come through  till the end of september but  this was actually one of the data  sources that i had  originally envisioned was fec federal  election commission data  on campaign spending um i wouldn't  probably wouldn't have looked  specifically at advertising  you know rather i think overall spending  would have been a fine proxy  i think there's a few problems with that  though is  one um you know from year to year you  get a lot of variation  in in cost um and it's also a very  non-linear thing so it's a little tough  to figure out how to model that  um there's also just a lot of  complexities around things like media  markets  um so you know depending on whether  you're  say say for a congressional district if  you're in an urban district versus  a rural one um your ad buying costs are  going to be totally different  you know like if you're running for  example if you're running in upstate new  york  for a congressional seat you're going to  be blanketing the airwaves with tv ads  if you're running in you know new york  city you're probably not running any tv  ads  um because you're sharing that market  with 20 or  30 or 40 other congressional districts  um so it really dilutes the impact  so it's a it's a bit tough um also the  other part is simply that  you know this will that would be a lot  of data to wrangle um fec filings are  notoriously  hard to work with and um  you know there's there's certainly data  sets that have been put together by  others  for previous elections um but i'd still  have to go and do that  for you know the present year and part  of the problem too was  um you know a lot of those reports only  go  through um for example you know if i was  doing this in october  i would get the report through september  30th and for all of the previous years  i'd have to go back and recalculate  those  with respect to the same date on the  previous cycles  so at the end of the day it's just one  of those things it could could have been  done but  that was a choice i definitely made for  the sake of reducing complexity  rather than adding it  all right thank you we have another  question from  top tyler potentially a bit speculative  but are there any data sources that you  think  will grow important just a mail-in  voting trend continues or goes  digital post pandemic are there any data  sources that don't exist  that would improve the model  specifically decreased variability  yeah so so mail-in voting is actually  really  really helpful for this um in general  in part because well there's a couple  things one  um you know people who vote by mail tend  to be pretty  consistent um and so  for example you know if you like if  you're somebody who's voted by mail  in the past um you're you're much more  likely to do it in the future than if  you  uh say voted in person that's less of a  solid predictor  um i think the big question is going to  be  whether the trend towards mailing voting  is a uniquely 2020 thing or if it  becomes more permanent because places  like colorado for example that have  switched  over the past decade to all vote by mail  um they've tended to see  you know much more consistent high rates  of turnout um  the problem we had this year was you  know not knowing how to account for the  switch to mail-in voting  excuse me um for the switch to high  rates of mail and voting  because of the pandemic context  excuse me but the other thing i was  thinking about with this is  mail-in voting does give you two  advantages when it comes to prediction  and again  i couldn't take advantage of it this  year because we didn't have good  precedent from previous years  to know how much this was going to  affect turnout but  one is that in order to vote by mail you  need to be registered ahead of time  um one of the tough things for  forecasting uh good for turnout bad for  forecasting over the past couple decades  is the move to same day registration in  more and more states  which is great because it removes a  barrier to voting but it's also tough  because the number of registered voters  is a less valuable signal than it used  to be because  you know back in the day where you had  to register say 30 days out  you at least knew what the denominator  is on the total votes  um and and that's not something you know  with same day voting because or same day  registration because a lot of people  might show up who aren't on the voter  rolls at all that you don't know about  um with voting by mail you know you do  have to be registered ahead of time  and so that does give you a useful  signal not only of the total number but  for any individual  um one of the really helpful things  about a voter registration is it can  tell you  is this person currently located in this  location  on an individual level things like you  know an updated voter registration is a  really important signal  but then um the other thing is you know  you also get  mail-in ballot requests and so you know  if i'm  in most places you know you can't just  print out a ballot online and mail it in  you have to mail in an application they  send you a ballot and then you send that  back in  so we know who's requested and been sent  a ballot already  um and that's a really strong signal as  well and it can tell you both  you know for an individual are they  likely to vote but also just what the  level of  enthusiasm is i think the challenge in  2020 was  you know again totally different context  and especially there were lots of states  that were  there there were some states that were  mailing everybody an absentee ballot  there were others like massachusetts  where they didn't mail everybody a  ballot  but they mailed every registered voter  in application and so they removed one  of the barriers that you previously  had so because of that requesting a  ballot was no longer  the same kind of signal that it was in  previous years um  so that was the kind of thing where  again you know like some of the other  competitiveness metrics  we were seeing signals that interest was  high the problem is  we just didn't have enough precedent to  say how that would translate to some  change in probability of voting or  changing the expected level of voting  and so i couldn't factor into the model  that again is the kind of  reason why we made that forecast that  allowed us to jump up to the high and  historic forecasts  um because we we knew that you know  turnout was almost certainly going to be  higher  than our baseline prediction we just  didn't have a good way to say how much  higher and and  how that might vary from place to place  uh all right excellent we'll give the  last question to drew  do you think this model could be used or  adapted to detect  voter suppression or intimidation  i would be very very concerned about  that and that was something i saw a lot  of problems with  um on election night and and  in the aftermath where um you know a lot  of  a lot of the claims about voting  irregularities  uh often came about because people were  looking at  the um the aps forecasts which most news  organizations were using  and saying hey you know why why do we  have  too many votes or not enough votes like  the numbers don't line up  without recognizing and this is part of  that problem with  the black box model that i was trying to  avoid with  all the documentation people were  assuming that those were actual counts  of balance  and so that somehow the you know biden  plus trump number  didn't match the the total count and  that there was something wrong there  when in fact  those are two separate things it was  just simply that the predictions were  off  and i would be really really nervous to  use this for things around voter  suppression or intimidation  um because you would need something  happening at a massive scale  you know for it to be detectable because  like i was saying with my my model  i expect variation that's plus or minus  five percent  to happen you know ten or twenty percent  of the time  at least um i expect variation that's  you know seven or eight or ten percent  off from the prediction to happen many  places  you know not not a lot of the time but  maybe one percent of the time two  percent  and those so so when those things are  normal  um you would need a massive effect  of voter suppression or intimidation for  it to be meaningful so  you know if i see the model as wrong  compared to the actual  real world results um my takeaway isn't  that there's a problem with the results  my  takeaway is that well that's just the  model being wrong  i think a lot of other data scientists  particularly in politics are really bad  at remembering to have that kind of  humility  but but my first take is always that you  know if the model  and the actual real world data don't  line up um  more likely than not that's the model's  problem not the real world data so  you know in this case i would be a  little hesitant to try to use it for  those circumstances  um i mean you could always use it as a  way to start and i think  going back to that 2014 example um  when i was at the dnc you know we were  using the election night  um incoming data compared to the  forecast  to look for signs of irregularities in  that case for example  um one that we were looking at you know  we it was a county in maryland where we  thought they actually just  had the numbers backwards between the  democrat and republican  because our you know our forecast was  like 60 40  and the results were coming back 40 60.  um  turns out that county just really hated  our candidate  i still don't understand quite why but  like that those numbers were right we  were just off by 20 points  um and we had sent in the lawyers to  literally inquire about this the dnc's  lawyers  which i think was a good use case but i  think it's also a great example of why  um even when you see something as as  seemingly wrong as the numbers being off  by 20 points  you know that happens especially when  you're looking across a large number of  geographies  so i'd be really worried about using it  for that use case  uh all right uh  do any of your you or any other  organizers have anything else to add  not really i just want to thank  everybody for coming and thank everybody  for staying i know we've been going  almost two hours and i see almost  everybody has stuck around  so i'm very excited to see that and and  you know  thanks to you as well and everybody else  who helped organize this  um really great to have more people  contributing
 

